## 1. Modelling Approach and Rationale
 
### 1.1 The core constraint: this is a pre-publication forecast
 
ViewCastLK predicts how a video will perform **before it is published**. At prediction time the
video does not exist on YouTube yet. There are no day-1 views, no day-2 views, no early
engagement curve — only the metadata the creator is planning to use.
 
This single constraint determines the entire modelling approach and must be understood by everyone
on the team, because it rules out a class of methods that would otherwise be natural for this
problem.
 
### 1.2 Why not classical time-series forecasting
 
Classical time-series methods (ARIMA, SARIMA, Prophet) and sequence models (LSTM, GRU,
Transformers) forecast future values of a series **from past values of that same series**. They
require a history of the target variable as input.
 
For ViewCastLK that history does not exist at prediction time. A model that needs "views on day 1
through day 6" in order to predict day 7 cannot serve a creator who has not published yet. It
would be solving a different problem: *trajectory refinement for an already-published video*.
 
That is a legitimate and interesting problem, and it is recorded in Section 11 as a possible
version 2 feature. It is explicitly **out of scope for version 1**.
 
### 1.3 Chosen approach: XGBoost multi-output regression
 
The chosen approach is a gradient-boosted decision tree model (XGBoost) trained on **pre-publication
tabular features only**, producing four outputs simultaneously:
 
- `d7_views` — predicted view count 7 days after publication
- `d14_views` — predicted view count 14 days after publication
- `d21_views` — predicted view count 21 days after publication
- `d30_views` — predicted view count 30 days after publication
This is justified because:
 
- The feature space is tabular, mixed-type (numeric, boolean, categorical) and moderate in size —
  exactly the regime where gradient-boosted trees are the strongest default.
- XGBoost handles missing values natively, which matters because the dashboard allows optional
  inputs (Section 9).
- Feature importances are directly interpretable, which the project needs for the insights layer
  and for the ablation study (Section 8.4).
- The dataset is in the hundreds-to-low-thousands of videos. Deep sequence models would be
  data-starved at this scale; boosted trees will not be.
### 1.4 One multi-output model, not four separate models
 
**Decision: a single multi-output model with four target columns.**
 
The four horizons are strongly correlated with each other — a video at 10,000 views on day 7 will
almost certainly exceed that by day 14. This correlation is *helpful*, not a problem: the shared
model structure lets the four horizons reinforce each other rather than being learned from scratch
four times.
 
Practical benefits:
 
- One training run, one artefact to version, one artefact to deploy.
- Consistent feature handling across horizons — no risk of the day-7 model and the day-30 model
  silently diverging in preprocessing.
- Videos with incomplete tracking can still contribute to the horizons they *do* have
  (Section 5.3), which a four-separate-models setup makes messier to manage.
Implementation note for Claude Code: use `xgboost.XGBRegressor` with
`multi_strategy="one_output_per_tree"` (or `MultiOutputRegressor` as a fallback if the installed
XGBoost version predates native multi-output support). Both are acceptable; the wrapper choice is
an implementation detail, not a design decision.
 
