| `title_curiosity_gap` | Title via Gemini | 0–10, see Section 6 |
 
### 4.3 Features explicitly dropped
 
| Feature | Reason for dropping |
|---|---|
| `channel_id` | Identifier. Would let the model memorise specific channels instead of learning transferable patterns, and cannot generalise to a channel not in training |
| `category_id` | Redundant with `category_name`, and numeric IDs imply a false ordering |
| `published_at` | Raw timestamp. Its useful content is already extracted into hour/day-of-week/weekend features. Feeding the raw timestamp would let the model learn "later dates get fewer recorded views", which is a collection artefact, not a real effect |
| `definition` (HD/SD) | Effectively constant in the modern data; viewers do not filter on it |
| `caption` | Low signal, high missingness; most uploads have no captions |
| `default_language` | Near-duplicate of `default_audio_language`. **Keep exactly one** — whichever has fewer nulls in the collected data. Verify before training |
| `channel_country` | Effectively constant (the entire dataset is scoped to Sri Lanka-based channels). Zero-variance features add nothing |
| `d*_hours_off`, `d*_usable`, `eligible`, `is_live_broadcast`, `ch_stats_as_of` | Data-quality metadata, used for row filtering and target validation only — never as model inputs |
 
### 4.4 Categorical encoding: target encoding, not one-hot
 
**Decision: target encoding for `category_name`.**
 
One-hot encoding the 15 YouTube categories would add 15 sparse binary columns to a feature set
that is already around 20 columns wide, on a dataset of low thousands of rows. That worsens the
rows-per-feature ratio for no gain.
 
Target encoding replaces `category_name` with a **single numeric column** holding the mean target
value for that category. Because there are four targets, four encoded columns are produced
(`category_enc_d7`, `category_enc_d14`, `category_enc_d21`, `category_enc_d30`) — still far leaner
than 15 sparse columns, and each carries real signal.
 
**Leakage protection (mandatory):**
 
- Encoding values are computed on the **training split only**.
- Use the **log mean** (`mean of log1p(views)`) rather than the raw mean, so that one viral video
  does not dominate its category's encoding.
- Apply **smoothing** toward the global mean for categories with few training videos:
  `encoded = (n * category_mean + k * global_mean) / (n + k)`, with `k` around 10. This prevents a
  sparse category such as Nonprofits & Activism from being encoded off a handful of rows.
- The resulting category → value mapping is **saved as an artefact** alongside the model. Test-set
  rows and live dashboard requests both look up this frozen mapping. They never recompute it.
- Any category unseen at inference falls back to the global mean.
This last point also answers the concern raised during design about the dashboard: *the creator
never sees or enters an encoded value.* They pick "Music" from a dropdown; the backend looks up the
stored mapping. The same applies to every derived feature — see Section 9.3.
 
### 4.5 Two decisions worth recording explicitly
 
**Duration is kept continuous and is *not* pre-bucketed.**
Gradient-boosted trees perform their own binning at every split, and they choose the cut points
from the data. Manually bucketing into "under 30 s / under 5 min / under 10 min" throws away
resolution and hard-codes cut points that may be wrong for this market. Feed the raw seconds.
 
**`is_short` is kept anyway, alongside `duration_seconds`.**
This is not redundancy. YouTube Shorts are served through a separate surface with different
discovery and retention dynamics — a 55-second Short and a 55-second regular upload are different
products with the same duration. The boolean encodes format, not length.
 
### 4.6 Channel ratio features — to be tested, not assumed
 
Three interpretable ratios can be derived from the four raw channel snapshots without any
historical channel data:
 
| Ratio | Formula | Interpretation |
|---|---|---|
| `ch_videos_per_day` | `ch_videos_at_publish / channel_age_days_at_publish` | Upload frequency / channel activity level |
| `ch_views_per_video` | `ch_views_at_publish / ch_videos_at_publish` | Historical average performance per upload |
| `ch_views_per_sub` | `ch_views_at_publish / ch_subs_at_publish` | Engagement efficiency relative to audience size |
 
**Decision: generate all three, then let the data decide.** They are included in the correlation
analysis (Section 8.1) and in the ablation study (Section 8.4). Whichever ratios fail to improve
the model are dropped and the removal is documented.
 
Two points that were raised during design and are resolved here:
 
- *Subscriber growth rate* is **not available** and is not attempted. It would require channel
  snapshots from before the collection window, which do not exist. It is listed in Section 11 as a
  future extension once `channel_snapshots` has accumulated enough history.
- *PCA on the channel features* is **rejected.** PCA would compress the four channel columns into
  unnamed components, destroying exactly the interpretability the project needs for its insights
  layer and its ablation study. Four correlated but interpretable columns are strictly better here
  than two uninterpretable ones. Correlation between features is not a problem for tree models in
  the first place.
- The objection that "the user cannot compute these ratios by hand" **does not apply**, because the
  dashboard auto-pulls channel statistics from the YouTube API (Section 9.2). The backend computes
  the ratios; the creator never touches them.
