## 6. LLM Title Scoring
 
### 6.1 Motivation
 
The title is the single strongest lever a creator controls, and the existing title features
(`title_has_question`, `title_upper_ratio`, and so on) capture only surface form. They cannot tell
the difference between an urgent title and a calm one, or a curiosity-driven title and a plain
descriptive one. Scoring the *tone* of the title adds a dimension no other feature covers.
 
### 6.2 Dimensions scored
 
Four dimensions, each scored **0–10**:
 
| Dimension | What it measures |
|---|---|
| `title_urgency` | Time pressure — "breaking", "now", "today", "last chance" |
| `title_emotional_appeal` | Emotional intensity — anger, joy, sadness, outrage, sympathy |
| `title_seriousness` | Register — formal and factual at the high end, playful and casual at the low end |
| `title_curiosity_gap` | How much the title withholds. Covers both explicit withholding ("you won't believe what happened next") and implicit promising ("5 reasons why...", "this one trick..."). The withheld-information case is one *type* of curiosity gap, not the whole of it |
 
These four are deliberately not orthogonal — urgency and emotional appeal overlap on some titles,
and seriousness anti-correlates with both on others. That overlap is acceptable: tree models are
untroubled by correlated inputs, and the correlation analysis will quantify how much redundancy
actually exists.
 
### 6.3 What is deliberately NOT scored — ethics position
 
**No score is produced for divisiveness, communal or ethnic provocation, controversy potential, or
clickbait/misleading-claim strength.**
 
This is a considered decision, and it is stated in the final report as an ethics section.
 
The reasoning: titles that inflame ethnic or religious tension, or that make deliberately
misleading claims, very likely *do* attract higher view counts in this market. That makes them
predictive. It does not make them appropriate to build a feature for. A feature named
"divisiveness score" turns a forecasting tool into an optimisation engine for exactly the content
that damages public discourse — and the tool would then be recommending it to creators.
 
The distinction the project holds to is:
 
> **The model may learn patterns from whatever exists in the data. The tool never recommends
> sensationalism or communal provocation to a creator.**
 
`title_emotional_appeal` captures emotional intensity in general, which is legitimate and neutral.
The recommendation layer (Section 9.4) is built around sustainable levers — genuine curiosity
hooks, clear framing, timing, format — never around outrage or misleading claims.
 
### 6.4 Implementation
 
| Aspect | Decision |
|---|---|
| Provider | Google Gemini, free tier. Budget for the project is effectively zero and must remain so |
| Call structure | **One call per title returning all four scores.** Four separate calls per title would quadruple token use and hit free-tier rate limits during backfill |
| Output format | Strict JSON: `{"urgency": 0-10, "emotional_appeal": 0-10, "seriousness": 0-10, "curiosity_gap": 0-10}`. The prompt instructs the model to return JSON only, with no prose and no code fences |
| Prompt content | Must state that titles may be in Sinhala, Tamil, English or a mix, and that scores should reflect the title as a Sri Lankan viewer would read it |
| Parsing | Strip any stray fences, parse, validate that all four keys are present and numeric and in range. Failed parses are retried once, then recorded as null |
| Backfill | All ~6,870 already-collected videos are scored in a batched, rate-limit-aware run |
| Ongoing | New videos are scored inside the daily collection pipeline, as each day's videos are discovered |
| Live inference | When a creator submits a title on the dashboard, Gemini is called in real time for that one title. At roughly 10 predictions per day this is negligible against the free tier |
| Failure handling | If the live call fails or times out, the four scores are passed as missing. XGBoost handles missing values natively, so a prediction is still returned — degraded, not broken |
 
### 6.5 Storage — one schema recommendation
 
The four scores were provisionally to be stored on `video_snapshots`. **Recommendation: store them
on the `videos` table instead** (or on a dedicated `video_title_scores` table keyed by video ID).
 
The reason is that a title's tone scores are a **static property of the video**, not a
time-varying measurement. `video_snapshots` holds one row per video per poll — storing the scores
there would duplicate the same four numbers across roughly 14 rows per video for no benefit, and
would create the risk of the copies disagreeing after a re-score.
 
This is a small change and is worth raising with the pipeline owner before backfill begins, since
correcting it afterwards means a migration. If the team prefers to keep them on
`video_snapshots`, the model layer can work with either — the `ml_video_features` view just needs
to expose one value per video.
 
