## 7. Data Splitting
 
**Decision: time-based split. Random k-fold cross-validation is not used.**
 
### 7.1 The split
 
Videos are ordered by `published_at`. The earlier portion becomes the training set; the later
portion becomes the test set. Indicative proportion: the first ~75–80 % of the collection window
for training, the final ~20–25 % for testing, with the exact date boundary fixed once the
collection window closes and recorded in the report.
 
A validation slice is carved from the end of the training portion (again by date, not randomly)
for hyperparameter tuning and early stopping, so that the test set stays untouched until final
evaluation.
 
### 7.2 Why not random k-fold
 
Random shuffling would place videos published on the same day, and often by the same channel, on
both sides of the split. The model would then be scored partly on its ability to recall
conditions it had already seen — a platform-wide traffic spike, a trending news event, a channel's
recent surge — rather than on its ability to forecast genuinely unseen future content.
 
The time-based split matches how the tool is actually used: a model trained on the past, asked
about a video that has not happened yet. It gives a lower, more honest accuracy figure, and it is
the defensible choice in the report.
 
### 7.3 Interaction with target encoding
 
The category target-encoding map is computed **inside the training split only**, after the split is
made. This ordering is not optional — computing it on the full dataset first would leak test-period
outcomes into training features and silently inflate every metric.
 
