## 8. Evaluation
 
### 8.1 Correlation analysis before training
 
Before any model is trained, produce a correlation matrix covering every candidate feature against
each of the four targets (on the log scale), plus feature-to-feature correlations.
 
Purpose:
 
- Confirm — rather than assume — that the LLM tone scores actually relate to viewership. Intuition
  says emotive titles draw views; the data may show that urgency matters far more than seriousness,
  or that curiosity gap is weaker in this market than expected. It is a short piece of work and it
  prevents building the model around a weak signal.
- Identify redundant pairs (correlation above roughly 0.9) — `title_length` against
  `title_word_count` is the obvious candidate, and `ch_subs_at_publish` against
  `ch_views_at_publish` is likely.
- Produce figures for the EDA section of the final report.
Redundant features are not automatically dropped — the ablation study (8.4) makes that call — but
they are flagged here.
 
### 8.2 The naive baseline (build this first)
 
**The baseline is built and measured before the XGBoost model.**
 
Baseline definition: for a video in category *C*, predict the **median** (not mean) log-scale view
count of training-set videos in category *C* at each horizon, inverted back to the view scale.
Median is used because a single viral video would otherwise drag a category's mean far above what
a typical video in that category achieves.
 
Two variants are measured so the report can show the effect of channel size:
 
1. **Category-only baseline** — the naive baseline named in the proposal.
2. **Category × channel-tier baseline** — the same, split by the mega/mid/small subscriber tier
   already assigned at ingest. This is a *harder* baseline to beat and makes the XGBoost result
   more credible.
The baseline MAPE is what defines success. It is not known yet, and it will not be guessed.
 
### 8.3 Metrics
 
| Metric | Role |
|---|---|
| **MAPE, combined across all four horizons** | Primary headline metric, as specified in the proposal |
| **MAPE, per horizon (d7 / d14 / d21 / d30)** | Reported alongside the combined figure. Combined MAPE alone hides the fact that d30 will be weaker than d7, both because it is intrinsically harder and because fewer rows have a usable d30 target |
| R² | Variance explained |
| MAE | Absolute error magnitude in views |
| RMSE | Penalises large misses; reported for completeness |
 
All metrics are computed on the **original view-count scale**, after inverting the log transform.
 
**On a target MAPE figure.** A 10 % MAPE target was raised early in design. It should not be
adopted, and here is the honest reasoning: individual video performance on YouTube is driven
substantially by factors this model cannot observe — thumbnail quality, algorithmic promotion,
external sharing, timing luck. Published work on social-media popularity prediction does not reach
10 % on individual-item view forecasting. Setting that target guarantees the report ends with an
apparent failure even if the model is genuinely good.
 
Realistic expectations, to be confirmed against the measured baseline:
 
- Naive category baseline: roughly **40–60 % MAPE**
- XGBoost with the full feature set: plausibly **25–35 % MAPE**
**The success criterion is relative, not absolute:** a meaningful, quantified reduction in MAPE
versus the naive baseline, consistent across all four horizons. This is what the proposal already
committed to, and it is the criterion that will be defended in the report.
 
### 8.4 Ablation study
 
The ablation study quantifies what each feature group actually contributes. It is a required
deliverable, not optional.
 
Method: train the full model, record its metrics, then retrain with one feature group removed at a
time and record the change in MAPE.
 
| Configuration | Question it answers |
|---|---|
| Full model | Reference point |
| Minus LLM title scores | Did the Gemini integration earn its place? |
| Minus all title features (LLM and surface) | How much does the title matter at all? |
| Minus channel ratio features (6.4) | Should the engineered ratios be kept? |
| Minus raw channel snapshots | How much of the forecast is just "big channel gets big numbers"? |
| Minus publish-timing features | Is timing a real lever in this market, or folklore? |
| Minus category encoding | Does category carry signal beyond channel size? |
 
Results are presented as a table of MAPE deltas, plus XGBoost's own feature importances (gain-based,
and SHAP values if time permits). The timing and title ablations feed directly into the
dashboard's recommendation layer — a recommendation should only be shown to creators if the
ablation shows the underlying feature actually matters.
 
