## 9. Dashboard Integration
 
### 9.1 Input contract
 
| Field | Required | Control | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| Category | Yes | Dropdown, 15 YouTube categories | |
| Duration | Yes | Numeric input, converted to seconds | |
| Title | Yes | Text input | Sent to Gemini in real time for tone scores |
| Default audio language | Yes | Dropdown (Sinhala / Tamil / English / other) | |
| Made-for-kids | Yes | Toggle | |
| Channel URL or channel ID | Yes | Text input | Used to auto-pull channel statistics |
| Publish day of week | **Optional** | Dropdown | If omitted, the tool recommends the best day instead |
| Publish hour | **Optional** | Time picker, mapped to the SLT bucket | If omitted, the tool recommends the best time slot instead |
 
**Requirement: the model must return a prediction when optional fields are absent.** This is
satisfied without extra work — omitted fields are passed as missing values and XGBoost routes them
through its learned default direction at each split. No imputation step is required, and none
should be added.
 
### 9.2 Channel statistics are auto-pulled, never typed
 
The creator supplies a channel URL or ID. The backend calls `channels.list` (1 quota unit) and
retrieves subscriber count, total view count, video count and channel creation date, from which
`channel_age_days_at_publish` is computed.
 
At an expected volume of roughly 10 predictions per day this consumes on the order of 10 quota
units daily against the 10,000-unit free allowance — entirely negligible, and it removes a whole
class of manual-entry errors.
 
### 9.3 The feature-parity requirement
 
**The dashboard backend must apply exactly the same transformations, in exactly the same order, as
the training pipeline.** A mismatch here is the single most common way a model that scored well in
a notebook produces nonsense in production.
 
Concretely, the inference path must:
 
1. Look up `category_encoded` from the **saved encoding artefact** — never recompute it.
2. Map publish hour into the same SLT bucket boundaries used in training.
3. Compute `publish_is_weekend` from the selected day.
4. Derive the surface title features (`title_has_number`, `title_has_question`, `title_has_exclaim`,
   `title_upper_ratio`, `title_script`, `title_word_count`) using the **same code** the pipeline
   uses — shared module, not a reimplementation.
5. Call Gemini for the four tone scores.
6. Compute any retained channel ratios from the auto-pulled statistics.
7. Assemble the feature vector in the **same column order** the model was trained on.
Mitigation: the preprocessing logic is written once as a shared, importable module used by both
training and serving. It is not duplicated. A test asserting that a known training row, passed
through the serving path, reproduces its training feature vector exactly is worth the fifteen
minutes it takes to write.
 
### 9.4 Output
 
**Forecast.** All four horizons are shown as point predictions:
 
> "Based on this metadata, this video is projected to reach approximately X views by day 7,
> Y by day 14, Z by day 21 and W by day 30."
 
Presented as a curve with the four points marked, in plain language, without statistical notation.
 
**No confidence intervals in version 1.** This is a deliberate scope decision. XGBoost does not
produce prediction intervals natively; obtaining them would require either quantile regression
(additional models, additional training time) or post-hoc interval construction from test-set
residuals. Neither is justified before the point forecast itself is solid. Intervals are recorded
as a version 2 item (Section 11).
 
**Recommendations.** Every recommendation shown must be backed by a feature that survived the
ablation study:
 
- **Timing** — best publishing day and time slot for the selected category and channel tier,
  shown especially when the creator left those fields blank.
- **Title** — concrete suggestions derived from the tone scores of the submitted title, framed
  around genuine curiosity and clarity. Never around outrage, communal provocation or misleading
  claims (Section 6.3).
- **Duration** — whether the planned length sits inside the range that historically performs well
  for the chosen category.
- **Format** — whether Shorts or standard uploads perform better in this category for a channel of
  this size.
Each recommendation is accompanied by the evidence behind it ("videos in this category published
on Sunday evenings reached on average N % more views by day 7"), so the tool teaches rather than
just instructs.
 
